INT. GRAND HOTEL LOBBY - DAY

Marble pillars. A revolving door that sighs with each turn. A brass bell on the concierge desk. A poodle in a rhinestone collar naps under a velvet bench.

CORNELIUS "CORNY" BELLWEATHER (50s, three-piece suit, monocle) sits in a chair and pretends to read a newspaper. Across from him on a sofa, his wife PRUDENCE (40s, enormous hat, fan) watches him with mounting suspicion. At the desk, the concierge LEOPOLDINA ARCHAMBAULT-DIOP (30s, immaculate, bored) files her nails.

She shifts. He shifts. The poodle yawns.

PRUDENCE
Corny, you're holding the paper upside down.

CORNY
Am I?

PRUDENCE
The pictures are standing on their heads.

CORNY
Modern art.

She snaps her fan. A telephone on the desk rings, and Leopoldina answers in a voice like warm butter.

LEOPOLDINA
Front desk. Yes, Mr. Bellweather is in the lobby. No, he is not expecting a call.

Corny rises so quickly the newspaper flies. He crosses the lobby in three long strides and tries to take the phone from her hand.

LEOPOLDINA (CONT'D)
(covering the receiver)
Sir, it's for the hotel.

CORNY
(whispering)
It's for me.

PRUDENCE
(from the sofa)
Corny, who is it?

CORNY
Nobody! Wrong number!

CALLER (V.O.)
(over phone, faint)
Corny? It's Fifi. Are you coming up?

Silence. Leopoldina's eyebrow rises half an inch. Prudence stands. The poodle wakes.

PRUDENCE
Corny.

CORNY
She's the dog groomer.

PRUDENCE
At the Grand Hotel.

CORNY
They have a salon.

PRUDENCE
They do not.

LEOPOLDINA
We do not.

Corny, defeated, takes the receiver. He puts it to his ear and listens a long moment, then speaks.

CORNY
(into phone)
I'll be right up. Tell her to bring the poodle.

Prudence follows his gaze to the dog under the bench. It wags its tail. Leopoldina hangs up and, with perfect composure, lifts the brass bell and rings it once.
